 data-testid=3D"expandable-text-box">JinjaMonogatari =E2=80=93 Platform Dev=
elopment Update (Week 4)

Week 4 focused on expanding the backend and connecting the mobile applicati=
on to fetch from the API. The primary goal this week was completing the shr=
ine domain and building read endpoints to support the core discovery experi=
ence.

This week=E2=80=99s progress (API + Mobile):
Shrine API development:
- Designed and implemented shrine read endpoints for map, preview, and deta=
iled shrine views
- Added query =E2=86=92 handler =E2=86=92 service flow aligned with CQRS st=
ructure
- Implemented DTO-driven responses to minimize payload sizes and control ex=
posed data
- Integrated PostGIS spatial support for shrine locations and future distan=
ce filtering
Database &amp; infrastructure:
- Completed shrine-related entities, relationships, and configurations
- Handled migrations with schema expansion
- Added automatic timestamp handling and location synchronization
- Improved overall database structure for scalability
Etiquette system refinement:
- Refactored etiquette services and DTOs for cleaner, more secure responses
- Updated mobile data adapters to match new minimal API contracts
Mobile integration:
- Replaced mock shrine content with live API data
- Connected shrine screens to backend endpoints
- Resolved UI rendering issues affecting image display

Key lessons
Building these endpoints revealed how important clear API boundaries and ca=
refully designed DTOs are. Having clean contracts between mobile and backen=
d reduced complexity when integrating the API and debugging issues.

Next focus
- User shrine collections and bookmark persistence
- Location-based distance queries and search filtering
- Caching strategy across shrine pages
- Standardized loading, empty, and error states throughout the app

This week continued the transition toward a production-ready platform, with=
 the API now serving as the source of truth for shrine exploration.=20

