 Platform Development Update (Week 3)

Week 3 focused on developing JinjaMonogatari into a real full stack system.=
 The biggest shift this week was beginning to replace fixtures with live in=
frastructure and implementing authentication.

JinjaMonogatari is a cultural exploration platform that helps users discove=
r Shinto shrines through location, history, folklore, and citation-backed r=
esearch. The platform combines a public mobile app, a research-oriented CMS=
, and a shared API.

This week=E2=80=99s progress (API + Mobile):
Backend foundation:
- Set up a new .NET Web API with EF Core and PostgreSQL
- Implemented migrations and basic auto-seeding
- Added structured layering (Api / Application / Domain / Infrastructure)
- Introduced CQRS-style command/query separation
- Added global exception handling middleware
Authentication system:
- Implemented user registration and login with hashed passwords
- Added JWT token generation
- Created /users/me endpoint for authenticated user data
- Connected mobile app to live auth endpoints
- Protected profile routes for unauthenticated users
Etiquette system integration:
- Implemented Etiquette tab
- Moved etiquette content from fixtures to API-backed data
- Built API adapters and hooks for live data fetching
- Refined data models for steps, citations, and images
UI work:
- Implemented Login, Register, and Profile screens
- Began handling basic loading states and errors

Key lessons
Moving from local data to a live API opens up a new can of worms. Handling =
auth, sessions, error states, latency issues, and more introduces new compl=
exities that simple fixtures cant simulate. This is a big accomplishment, b=
ut its a reminder of how far I have left with this capstone.=20

Next focus
- Refresh tokens and session stability
- Saved shrines (user &lt;-&gt; shrine relationships)
- Loading and error state coverage across the app
- Continued API expansion for shrine data

