pment Update (Week 2)

Week 2 of implementation was mostly focused on turning mockups into functio=
nal features and improving the UI/UX.

JinjaMonogatari is a cultural exploration platform that helps users discove=
r Shinto shrines through location, history, folklore, and citation-backed r=
esearch. The system combines a public mobile app with a research-oriented C=
MS and shared API.

This week=E2=80=99s progress (Mobile + CMS):
Custom shrine markers:
	- Implemented custom map icons with press states
	- Resolved scaling and anchor issues for accurate placement
UI &amp; navigation polish:
	- Implemented tab icons
	- Adjusted map tile styling via MapTiler for smoother thematic styling
	- Fixed layering issues between popups and navigation
Shrine detail expansion:
	- Completed Kami, History, Folklore, and Gallery tabs
	- Built citation pipelines linking content, images, and sources
	- Implemented dynamic modals for gallery and folklore content
Architecture improvements:
	- Refactored mappers and data flow to keep my sanity
	- Introduced global design tokens and shared styles for consistency and DR=
Y code
Content Management System Planning:
	- Continued low-fidelity mockups and editor flow planning

Key lessons
UI behavior is very different across platforms. Designing for flexibility i=
nstead of fighting the framework saved time. Simplifying features where nec=
essary improved both stability and UX.

Next focus
- Etiquette screen
- Profile &amp; saved shrines
- Auth screens (login/signup)
- CMS mockups

