ogatari - Platform Development&nbsp;Update (Week 1)

I've officially started implementation on JinjaMonogatari, my capstone proj=
ect spanning both Software Development and Digital Media.

JinjaMonogatari is a research focused cultural exploration platform designe=
d to help users discover and understand Shinto shrines through location, hi=
story, folklore, and etiquette.

The system includes:
- a public mobile app for shrine discovery
- a research focused content management system (CMS) for curation and citat=
ion backed publishing
- a shared backend API delivering structured content to the app

This week I began developing the mobile app layer (demo video attached).

This week's progress:
- Established modular project structure, routing, and testing data
- Implemented a test user with dynamic distance calculations between user a=
nd shrines
- Integrated navigation from map/cards into shrine detail pages
- Built initial screens based on HiFi mockups

Challenges &amp; lessons:
Expo Go does not currently support MapLibre Native (the mapping stack I pla=
n to use). To keep momentum, I=E2=80=99m temporarily using the WebView impl=
ementation and will transition to native once core MVP features are functio=
nal.

I also encountered the differences between iOS and Android rendering (spaci=
ng, snapping, layout behavior). Navigating these has been a good introducti=
on into cross-platform mobile development.

Next focus:
Expanding shrine content tabs, refining UI interactions, and introducing cu=
stom shrine map markers.

I began researching and designing this system last semester, so it=E2=80=99=
s rewarding to see it come together.<button class=3D"e65317de _578c35fa _5b=
