njaMonogatari is a research-driven cultural exploration platform designed t=
o help users discover and understand Shinto shrines through location-aware =
exploration, historical context, folklore, and etiquette guidance.

Originally conceived as a UI/UX concept in a User-Centered Design course, t=
he project has evolved into a full software platform consisting of a produc=
tion-structured mobile application, a CQRS-based backend API, and a planned=
 editorial CMS for research and content publishing workflows.

The mobile application enables users to discover shrines through an interac=
tive map and curated learning experience, including structured shrine detai=
l views, citation-backed cultural content, location-based distance calculat=
ion, navigation integration, authentication, and personal shrine collection=
s.

The backend API is built with .NET, PostgreSQL, and PostGIS to deliver spat=
ially aware shrine data, authenticated user features, and scalable read mod=
els designed specifically for mobile consumption.

Upcoming development focuses on building the research-oriented CMS to suppo=
rt editorial workflows, citation management, and long-term content governan=
ce.

JinjaMonogatari reflects my interest in building software systems that comb=
ine thoughtful UX design, clean architecture, and structured data to connec=
t people with culture, history, and place in a respectful and educational w=
ay.<button class=3D"e65317de _578c35fa _5ba8eb7d _2071e158 f5b2f524 _692fac=
